Reason:
The variable le_tmp64 is declared without initialization, and then only 6 bytes are copied into it via memcpy, leaving the last two bytes uninitialized. This can lead to undefined behavior when le_tmp64 is later used in le64_to_cpu().

The code statements that fix the bug are:
__le64 le_tmp64 = 0;
Reason:
Initializing le_tmp64 to zero ensures that all 8 bytes have a defined value, preventing the use of uninitialized data. This eliminates the Smatch warning and ensures predictable behavior.

